    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 181811\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1111 AM PDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Dry conditions through the weekend with near normal temperatures\n\n* Chances of precipitation return early next week, followed by\n  mild weather.\n  \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Thursday.\n\nLingering shower activity in Lincoln Co looks to erode by early \nFriday morning with winds easing but still staying on the breezy \nside. Winds will increase into the afternoon but look to ease into \nthe night and become light through the weeknight as a ridging \npattern focuses over the west coast. This ridge will lead to region- \nwide drying with daytime relative humidities falling below 15 \npercent and overnight recoveries staying below 30 percent in the \ndesert valleys. Despite of the ridging pattern, high temperatures \nwon't move too far from average with most sites in the upper 90s to \nlow 100s. However, morning lows will continue the trend of staying \naround 5 degrees above average.\n\nThe ridging pattern quickly switches into a troughing pattern into \nthe work week. This will bring back breezy winds and chances for \nrain into the forecast by Monday afternoon. Chances for showers and \nstorms look to favor higher terrain that day, but could spread into \nthe desert valleys that evening and become more widespread by \nnightfall. However, the feed of moisture into the area looks iffy in \nsome model outputs, so this could be a situation for good chances \nfor rain,  but maybe not a lot of rainfall over time. It'll come \ndown to how deep and how tilted the trough becomes into Monday. \nBeyond that, longer term models put the region into a more neutral \npattern through the rest of the week. Some slight ridging and \ntroughing along with zonal patterns will affect the region over \nbrief periods next week, but won't steer the weather into any \nextremes. Some breezy afternoons with isolated storms will be \npossible along with temperatures remaining close to average through \nthe week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package... \nSoutherly winds (160 to 200 degrees true) are expected into the \nearly evening, with occasional gusts to around 30 knots possible. \nWinds will diminish after sunset, with directions remaining out of \nthe south to southwest into the early morning hours. Light and \nvariable winds are expected early Saturday morning, followed by a \nperiod of light north to northeast winds (020 to 060 degrees true) \nfrom late morning through early afternoon. Southeast to southwest \nwinds return by late tomorrow afternoon and evening, although speeds \nare expected to remain 10 knots or less. VFR conditions will prevail \nthrough the TAF period. \n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Southeasterly to \nsouthwesterly winds will continue across the region this afternoon, \nwith gusts of 20 to 30 knots possible into the early evening. One \nexception will be in the northern Owens Valley, including KBIH, \nwhere winds will likely remain northerly through the TAF period with \nspeeds staying below 10 knots. Another exception will be across the \nwestern Mojave Desert, including KDAG, where gusty westerly winds \nwill increase this evening and persist well into the night. All \nother locations should see winds diminish to 10 knots or less after \nsunset. VFR conditions will prevail in all areas through Saturday.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Murdock\nAVIATION...Planz\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
